\begin{proof}
Let $f(x)=x^m-x\in F[x]$, where $m=p^n$. Since $F$ has characteristic $p$, we have
\[
f'(x)=m x^{m-1}-1=p^n x^{p^n-1}-1=-1.
\]
Thus $f'(x)$ is a nonzero constant, so $f$ and $f'$ are relatively prime. A polynomial over a field has a multiple root in a splitting field if and only if it has a nontrivial common divisor with its derivative. Therefore $f$ has no multiple roots. Hence all roots of $x^m-x$ are distinct.\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Set $f(x)=x^m-x$, with $m=p^n$. In characteristic $p$, the element $m=p^n$ is zero in $F$, and consequently
\[
f'(x)=m x^{m-1}-1=-1\neq 0.
\]
Let $K$ be a splitting field of $f$ over $F$. If $\alpha$ were a repeated root of $f$ in $K$, then $f(\alpha)=f'(\alpha)=0$. But $f'(\alpha)=-1$, a contradiction. Therefore no root of $f$ is repeated in $K$, so all roots of $x^m-x$ are distinct.\end{proof}

